{
  "id": "v26-kind-labels",
  "asserts": "`frames.kind` is the CONTRACT's enumeration -- `function`, `coroutine`, `generator`, `async_generator` (§3) -- and what a reader PRINTS for it is the recorded language's word. On a TypeScript trace `coroutine` prints `[async]` and `async_generator` prints `[async generator]`, in `tree` and in `frame` alike, because JavaScript has no coroutines and `[coroutine]` would name a construct the program does not contain. `generator` is JavaScript's own word and is printed unchanged. The Python column maps nothing, so a Python trace still prints `[coroutine]` character for character -- pinned by the legacy suite (`tests/test_tree.py`, `tests/test_frame.py`), which is the fence around every Python string, so no second copy of this vector is needed to hold it. The fifth frame carries the same rule applied to a SITE rather than a word (R28): a `sensorium-ts` YIELD records no line, so a frame parked on one has a NULL `line`, and both `tree` and `frame` print the suspension WITHOUT a site -- `~ suspended at end of recording`, `state: suspended` -- never the literal `LNone`, which is a reader inventing a site out of an absent record. A Python or Rust suspension always carries a line and renders unchanged.",
